# 1. OLYMPIODORUS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BIOS["olympiodorus"] = """<p>Olympiodorus the Younger (c. 495–570 CE) was a Neoplatonic philosopher and teacher who served as the head of the philosophical school at Alexandria during the 6th century, and whose name is associated with a significant body of alchemical writing preserved in the Byzantine Greek alchemical manuscript tradition. He is to be distinguished from several other figures who bore the same name in antiquity, including Olympiodorus the Elder (fl. c. 412 CE), an Athenian Neoplatonist, and Olympiodorus the historian of the Western Empire (fl. 5th century CE). Olympiodorus the Younger was among the last representatives of the Alexandrian Neoplatonic tradition before the school's decline following Justinian's restrictions on pagan philosophy (529 CE). His surviving philosophical works — commentaries on Plato's <i>Alcibiades I</i>, <i>Gorgias</i>, <i>Phaedo</i>, and on Aristotle's <i>Meteorologica</i> and <i>Categories</i> — establish him as a significant transmitter of Neoplatonic philosophical teaching in a Christian-dominated Late Antique context. His association with the alchemical tradition derives from the attribution of a treatise <i>On the Sacred Art</i> (<i>Peri tēs hierās technēs</i>) to "Olympiodorus the philosopher" in the Byzantine alchemical manuscript corpus.</p>

<h2>Works and Intellectual Context</h2>
<p>Olympiodorus the Younger worked in the tradition established by Ammonius of Alexandria (c. 435/445–517/526 CE), whose school had successfully navigated the transition from pagan to Christian intellectual culture by focusing on philosophical commentary — particularly on Aristotle — rather than on pagan cult or theology. Olympiodorus continued this accommodating approach, producing commentaries that engaged seriously with Platonic and Aristotelian philosophy while operating in a Christianized institutional environment. His commentary on the <i>Phaedo</i> is particularly important: it preserves significant material on Neoplatonic psychology, the soul's relationship to the body, and the philosophical dimensions of death and the afterlife — themes directly relevant to the alchemical tradition's concerns with purification and transformation.</p>

<p>The alchemical text attributed to "Olympiodorus the philosopher" — edited by Marcellin Berthelot and Charles-Émile Ruelle in their monumental <i>Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs</i> (3 vols., Paris, 1887–1888) — is a commentary on the alchemical writings of pseudo-Democritus (<i>Physika kai Mystika</i>) and Zosimos of Panopolis. The text engages with the allegorical dimensions of alchemical practice — the "divine water," the tinctures, the transformation of metals — in terms drawn from Neoplatonic metaphysics, treating the alchemical process as an analogue of the philosophical soul's purification and ascent. Whether the author of this alchemical treatise is identical with Olympiodorus the Younger the philosopher, or whether the attribution is a later pseudepigraphic assignment designed to lend philosophical authority to alchemical writing, is a question that modern scholarship has not definitively resolved. Michela Pereira, Lawrence M. Principe, and other scholars of the Hellenistic and Byzantine alchemical tradition have noted the difficulty: the alchemical <i>On the Sacred Art</i> shows familiarity with the Neoplatonic philosophical tradition consistent with an Alexandrian school context, but this familiarity could as well reflect the broad education of a 5th or 6th-century Alexandrian intellectual as the specific authorship of the historical Olympiodorus.</p>

<h2>Hermetic Significance</h2>
<p>The Olympiodoran alchemical text, whatever its authorship, represents an important moment in the integration of Neoplatonic philosophical categories into the interpretation of alchemical practice. The author — referred to here as "Olympiodorus" following manuscript tradition without prejudging the identification question — treats the operations of the sacred art as philosophically serious activities whose significance goes beyond mere technical metallurgy. The transformation of base metals is read as an analogue of the Neoplatonic soul's purification: just as the soul must be stripped of its material accretions through philosophical discipline before it can return to its divine source, so the base metal must be dissolved, purified, and reconstituted through the operations of the alchemical art before it can achieve its perfect form. This allegorical reading does not deny the material reality of the operations — the text contains specific procedural instructions — but frames them within a cosmological and psychological interpretation that connects practical alchemy to the broader Hermetic-Neoplatonic tradition of Late Antiquity.</p>

<p>The text preserves significant material on Zosimos of Panopolis and pseudo-Democritus, serving as a crucial witness for the state of the Greek alchemical tradition in the 6th century. Its discussion of the "divine water" (<i>theion hydōr</i>) — the universal solvent and transforming agent of Greek alchemy — connects to the broader Hermetic pneumatological tradition in which water, spirit, and light are the primary agents of cosmic transformation. The text also preserves important information about the social organization of the alchemical tradition: the reference to alchemical "adepts" (<i>philosophoi</i>) who guard the secrets of the art from the uninitiated echoes the initiatory language of the Hermetic philosophical texts and of the mystery traditions of Late Antiquity.</p>

<h2>Transmission and Reception</h2>
<p>The alchemical text attributed to Olympiodorus was preserved in the Byzantine Greek alchemical manuscript tradition, the same corpus that preserved the writings of Zosimos, Synesius, and the pseudo-Democritean texts. The primary manuscripts are collected in Berthelot and Ruelle's <i>Collection</i> — the foundational scholarly edition of the Greek alchemical corpus, now partially superseded by more recent critical work but still indispensable. The philosophical commentaries of Olympiodorus the Younger were transmitted through the Byzantine philosophical tradition and have been edited in the Commentaria in Aristotelem Graeca series and in individual critical editions. The dual transmission — philosophical and alchemical — under the same name contributed to the subsequent conflation of the two bodies of work in Renaissance and early modern alchemical anthologies, where "Olympiodorus" appears as an authority on both Platonic philosophy and alchemical practice.</p>

<h2>Scholarly Debates</h2>
<p>The principal scholarly debate concerns the identification of the alchemical "Olympiodorus the philosopher" with the historical Olympiodorus the Younger of Alexandria. The Berthelot-Ruelle edition assumed the identification as probable; subsequent scholarship has been more cautious. The alchemical text's Neoplatonic philosophical vocabulary is consistent with Alexandrian school culture of the 5th–6th century, but this is consistent with multiple possible authors. Matteo Martelli's work on the Byzantine transmission of Greek alchemy and Robert Halleux's studies of the alchemical manuscript tradition have clarified the textual situation without definitively resolving the authorship question. The broader question of the relationship between the Alexandrian Neoplatonic school and the alchemical tradition — how much mutual awareness and influence existed between professional philosophers and alchemical practitioners in Late Antique Alexandria — remains an active area of research.</p>

<h2>Literature</h2>
<p>Berthelot, Marcellin and Charles-Émile Ruelle. <i>Collection des anciens alchimistes grecs</i>. 3 vols. Paris: Steinheil, 1887–1888.</p>
<p>Westerink, L. G. (ed.). <i>Olympiodorus: In Platonis Gorgiam commentaria</i>. Leipzig: Teubner, 1970.</p>
<p>Westerink, L. G. (ed.). <i>The Greek Commentaries on Plato's Phaedo</i>. Vol. 1: Olympiodorus. Amsterdam: North-Holland Publishing, 1976.</p>
<p>Principe, Lawrence M. <i>The Secrets of Alchemy</i>. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 2013.</p>
<p>Fowden, Garth. <i>The Egyptian Hermes: A Historical Approach to the Late Pagan Mind</i>. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1986.</p>
<p>Halleux, Robert. <i>Les textes alchimiques</i>. Turnhout: Brepols, 1979.</p>
<p>Hanegraaff, Wouter J. (ed.). <i>Dictionary of Gnosis and Western Esotericism</i>. 2 vols. Leiden: Brill, 2006.</p>
<p>Blumenthal, H. J. "529 and Its Sequel: What Happened to the Academy?" <i>Byzantion</i> 48 (1978): 369–385.</p>"""

