BIOS["suhrawardi"] = """<p>Shihab al-Din Yahya al-Suhrawardi (c. 1155–1191 CE), known as <i>Shaykh al-Ishraq</i> (Master of Illumination), was a Persian philosopher and mystic who founded the school of Illuminationist (<i>Ishraqi</i>) philosophy — one of the most significant intellectual traditions of medieval Islamic thought and one that explicitly incorporated the figure of Hermes Trismegistus into a prophetic chain of primordial wisdom. Born in Suhraward, in the northwestern Iranian province of Zanjan, he was educated in Maragha and Isfahan before traveling extensively through Anatolia and Syria. Executed in Aleppo under Saladin at approximately the age of 36 — the circumstances are disputed, probably involving both political and theological charges — he left a substantial body of philosophical and mystical writing that his students preserved and that formed the foundation of the Ishraqi tradition continued by figures including Mulla Sadra (1572–1640) and Qutb al-Din Shirazi (1236–1311). His importance for the history of Hermeticism lies in his explicit integration of Hermes Trismegistus — identified in the Islamic tradition with the Quranic prophet Idris — into a lineage of divine wisdom that runs from the primordial sages of Persia and Egypt through Greek philosophy to Islamic prophethood.</p>

<h2>Works and Intellectual Context</h2>
<p>Suhrawardi's philosophical corpus falls into two broad categories: the peripatetic (<i>mashayi</i>) treatises, written in a discursive Aristotelian style and dealing with logic, natural philosophy, and metaphysics; and the Illuminationist (<i>ishraqi</i>) works, written in a more mystical and symbolic register that draws on Platonic, Neoplatonic, and Iranian Zoroastrian sources. His masterpiece is <i>Hikmat al-Ishraq</i> (The Philosophy of Illumination, c. 1186 CE), a systematic philosophical work that begins with an Aristotelian critique of the theory of definition and proceeds to a complete metaphysical system grounded in the ontology of light. In Suhrawardi's system, existence is fundamentally luminous: God is the Light of Lights (<i>Nur al-Anwar</i>), and all beings are characterized by their degree of luminosity — their capacity to receive and transmit divine light. The system represents a deliberate synthesis of Platonic form theory, Neoplatonic emanationism, Zoroastrian light-cosmology, and Islamic Sufi mystical experience.</p>

<p>Related works include <i>Hayakil al-Nur</i> (The Temples of Light), a shorter philosophical meditation on the body as the temple of the luminous soul; <i>Al-Talwihat</i> (The Intimations), a systematic philosophical work in the peripatetic mode; and a series of mystical narratives (<i>qisas</i>) — including <i>The Red Intellect</i>, <i>The Rustling of Gabriel's Wing</i>, and <i>In the Land of Exile</i> — that use allegorical narrative and visionary imagery to convey the soul's journey toward illumination. These visionary narratives are particularly significant for the Hermetic tradition: they draw on the Iranian mythological geography of Hurqalya (the imaginal world intermediate between the material and the intelligible) and on the figure of the celestial guide who leads the soul upward — a figure that has deep structural affinities with the Hermetic guide-Poimandres of CH I.</p>

<h2>Hermetic Significance</h2>
<p>Suhrawardi's significance for the history of Hermeticism is multidimensional. Most directly, he explicitly incorporates Hermes Trismegistus — whom he identifies with Idris, the Quranic prophet of wisdom — into a chain of primordial sages (<i>hukama</i>) that stretches from the ancient Iranian sage-kings (Kayumarth, Faridun, Kay Khusraw) through Agathodaemon (identified with Seth or with the Greek hermetist), Asclepius, Pythagoras, Plato, and into the Islamic tradition. This wisdom chain (<i>silsila</i>) functions as the Illuminationist counterpart of the Hermetic <i>prisca theologia</i> — the claim of a primordial revelation transmitted through a chain of wise initiates. Kevin van Bladel's <i>The Arabic Hermes: From Pagan Sage to Prophet of Science</i> (2009) provides the definitive account of how Hermes came to be identified with Idris in the Islamic tradition, a development that makes Suhrawardi's synthesis possible.</p>

<p>John Walbridge's studies — particularly <i>The Leaven of the Ancients: Suhrawardi and the Heritage of the Greeks</i> (2000) and <i>The Wisdom of the Mystic East: Suhrawardi and Platonic Orientalism</i> (2001) — have provided the most sophisticated account of Suhrawardi's relationship to Greek philosophical sources, demonstrating that his engagement with Platonism and Neoplatonism was substantive and philosophically significant, not merely decorative. Suhrawardi's "Oriental philosophy" (<i>hikmat al-ishraq</i>) is not a simple appropriation of Western or Greek thought but a genuine synthesis in which Greek, Iranian, and Islamic elements are transformed into a new philosophical vision. The Hermetic element — via the Arabic Hermes tradition — is one constituent of this synthesis rather than its dominant component, but it is genuinely present and philosophically operative.</p>

<h2>Transmission and Reception</h2>
<p>Suhrawardi's Illuminationist philosophy had its most enduring impact in the Persian-speaking Islamic world, where it became one of the three main strands of Islamic philosophy alongside the Peripatetic (<i>mashayi</i>) tradition of Avicenna and the later speculative mysticism (<i>irfan</i>) of Ibn Arabi. Mulla Sadra (1572–1640), the greatest philosopher of the Safavid period, incorporated Ishraqi elements into his system of "Transcendent Wisdom" (<i>hikmat al-mutaaliya</i>) along with Avicennan philosophy and Ibn Arabian mysticism. In the Ottoman world, Suhrawardi's works were studied in the madrasa curriculum and were commented on by scholars including Mulla Lutfallah Khunaji. The reception of Suhrawardi in the modern West was shaped primarily by Henry Corbin (1903–1978), the French orientalist and philosopher whose four-volume <i>En Islam iranien</i> (1971–1972) and whose translations and editions of Suhrawardi's works gave the Ishraqi tradition its first sustained Western scholarly attention. Corbin's interpretation — deeply influenced by his own Heideggerian and phenomenological philosophical commitments — should be used critically, as Walbridge and others have noted, but it remains indispensable as an introduction to the tradition.</p>

<h2>Scholarly Debates</h2>
<p>The principal scholarly debates about Suhrawardi concern the sources and originality of his philosophical synthesis, and the appropriate interpretive framework for understanding his relationship to the Islamic mystical tradition. Corbin's reading emphasized the continuity between Suhrawardi and the Iranian mythological tradition, the angelology of Zoroastrianism, and the Shi'ite esotericism of the Imam — a reading that Walbridge has substantially qualified by insisting on the Greek philosophical dimensions of the Ishraqi system and by demonstrating that Suhrawardi's relationship to Zoroastrian sources is more selective and mediated than Corbin implied. Seyyed Hossein Nasr's presentations of Suhrawardi in <i>Three Muslim Sages</i> (1964) and related works offer an appreciative philosophical reading within the framework of perennial philosophy, which Hanegraaff's methodological framework would classify as an Analyst-level perspective that should be distinguished from strictly historical analysis. The political circumstances of Suhrawardi's execution remain incompletely understood: the charges likely involved accusations of heresy (for claiming to receive fresh prophetic revelation) combined with political intrigue in Saladin's court, but the relative weight of these factors is disputed.</p>

<h2>Literature</h2>
<p>Walbridge, John. <i>The Leaven of the Ancients: Suhrawardi and the Heritage of the Greeks</i>. Albany: SUNY Press, 2000.</p>
<p>Walbridge, John. <i>The Wisdom of the Mystic East: Suhrawardi and Platonic Orientalism</i>. Albany: SUNY Press, 2001.</p>
<p>Van Bladel, Kevin. <i>The Arabic Hermes: From Pagan Sage to Prophet of Science</i>. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2009.</p>
<p>Corbin, Henry. <i>En Islam iranien: Aspects spirituels et philosophiques</i>. 4 vols. Paris: Gallimard, 1971–1972.</p>
<p>Nasr, Seyyed Hossein. <i>Three Muslim Sages: Avicenna, Suhrawardi, Ibn Arabi</i>. Cambridge: Harvard University Press, 1964.</p>
<p>Suhrawardi. <i>The Philosophy of Illumination</i>. Translated and edited by John Walbridge and Hossein Ziai. Provo: Brigham Young University Press, 1999.</p>
<p>Hanegraaff, Wouter J. (ed.). <i>Dictionary of Gnosis and Western Esotericism</i>. 2 vols. Leiden: Brill, 2006.</p>
<p>Ziai, Hossein. <i>Knowledge and Illumination: A Study of Suhrawardi's Hikmat al-Ishraq</i>. Atlanta: Scholars Press, 1990.</p>"""

