BIOS["thomas_of_york"] = """<p>Thomas of York (fl. c. 1250–1282) was an English Franciscan philosopher, theologian, and encyclopedist whose vast synthetic work <i>Sapientiale</i> (c. 1256–1260) represents one of the most ambitious attempts to harmonize Aristotelian natural philosophy with Christian theology and with the broader tradition of ancient wisdom — including texts attributed to Hermes Trismegistus — in 13th-century scholastic thought. A member of the Oxford Franciscan school, Thomas worked within the tradition established by Robert Grosseteste and developed by Roger Bacon, combining serious engagement with the newly available Aristotelian and Arabic philosophical texts with a concern to maintain the priority of Christian theological truth. David Porreca's meticulous research on the medieval scholastic reception of Hermetic texts has established Thomas of York as one of the most significant early scholastic philosophers to cite Hermes Trismegistus as a named philosophical authority — deploying Hermetic material alongside Aristotle, Avicenna, and the Church Fathers in systematic philosophical argument.</p>

<h2>Works and Intellectual Context</h2>
<p>The <i>Sapientiale</i> — Thomas's principal surviving work, edited in critical studies by George Bridges and others — is a massive encyclopedic work in seven books covering the whole of natural philosophy, metaphysics, and theology as understood in the mid-13th century. The work's title (roughly, "Book of Wisdom") signals its synthetic ambition: Thomas intended to demonstrate that all the wisdom of antiquity — Greek, Arabic, and Hermetic — converged upon and was perfected by Christian theology. This was a common strategy in the high scholastic period, visible also in Albertus Magnus's encyclopedic works and in the theological synthesis of Thomas Aquinas, but Thomas of York's version is notable for the prominence it gives to explicitly Hermetic sources.</p>

<p>Thomas drew upon the Hermetic texts available to him in Latin: the <i>Asclepius</i> (preserved in the corpus of Apuleius), the <i>Liber XXIV Philosophorum</i> (the collection of philosophical propositions attributed to Hermes), and various shorter Hermetic sententiae preserved in florilegia and encyclopedic collections. He also had access to the Arabic-Latin alchemical tradition, which preserved additional Hermetic material alongside the philosophical Hermetic texts. Porreca's analysis of the <i>Sapientiale</i> identifies specific passages in which Thomas cites "Hermes" or "Trismegistus" as an authority on theological and metaphysical questions — including questions about the nature of God, the structure of creation, and the relationship between intellect and matter — placing these citations alongside patristic and Aristotelian references in systematic arguments. This use of Hermetic authority is significant: it demonstrates that in the Oxford Franciscan milieu of the mid-13th century, Hermetic texts were regarded as serious philosophical sources worthy of citation in the highest-level theological argumentation.</p>

<h2>Hermetic Significance</h2>
<p>Thomas of York's significance for the history of Hermeticism lies precisely in his role as evidence for the circulation and intellectual status of Hermetic texts in the Latin scholastic tradition before the Renaissance recovery of the Greek <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i>. The standard historiographical narrative sometimes implies that Hermeticism was essentially absent from medieval European thought, awaiting the rediscovery of the Greek texts by Ficino in 1463. Porreca's research on the <i>Hermes Latinus</i> tradition — and Thomas of York's <i>Sapientiale</i> is a crucial piece of this evidence — demonstrates that this narrative is incorrect: Hermetic texts, particularly the <i>Asclepius</i> and the <i>Liber XXIV Philosophorum</i>, circulated widely in the medieval Latin tradition and were taken seriously as philosophical authorities by major scholastic thinkers.</p>

<p>Thomas's use of the <i>Liber XXIV Philosophorum</i> is particularly significant. The text's famous second proposition — "God is an infinite sphere whose center is everywhere and circumference nowhere" — appears in the <i>Sapientiale</i> as part of an argument about divine omnipresence and incomprehensibility. By deploying this Hermetic formula alongside patristic and Aristotelian arguments, Thomas integrates Hermetic theological language into the mainstream of Latin scholastic theology in a way that demonstrates both the availability and the prestige of the Hermetic sources in the Oxford Franciscan context. The formula subsequently appeared in Alan of Lille, Albertus Magnus, and many other scholastic writers — its transmission being precisely the kind of phenomenon that Porreca's <i>Hermes Latinus</i> research program has traced.</p>

<h2>Transmission and Reception</h2>
<p>Thomas of York's <i>Sapientiale</i> had a modest but real circulation in the later 13th and 14th centuries. Its influence was primarily local — within the English Franciscan tradition — and it did not achieve the pan-European reception of Albertus Magnus's works or the <i>Summa theologiae</i> of Thomas Aquinas. The work survives in a small number of manuscripts and was not printed in the early modern period. Its interest for modern scholarship is primarily as evidence for the intellectual culture of the Oxford Franciscan school and as a source for the history of the <i>Hermes Latinus</i> tradition — the cumulative body of Latin Hermetic material available in the medieval period. Porreca's research program has brought Thomas of York into visibility as a significant data point in the mapping of medieval Hermetic reception.</p>

<h2>Scholarly Debates</h2>
<p>The principal scholarly questions about Thomas of York concern the extent and depth of his engagement with Hermetic sources — how much he actually knew about the Hermetic tradition as opposed to citing isolated Hermetic sententiae lifted from florilegia — and his relationship to the broader 13th-century synthesis of Christian theology with non-Christian philosophical sources. Porreca's work is the starting point for any serious engagement with Thomas's use of Hermetic material. The broader context is provided by Charles Burnett's research on the 12th-century translation movement and by Paolo Lucentini's work on the medieval reception of the <i>Liber XXIV Philosophorum</i>. The question of how Thomas situates Hermetic authority in relation to Aristotelian, Augustinian, and Avicennan authority within his synthetic framework — whether Hermetic material is genuinely integrated or merely decorative — is a question that warrants further investigation.</p>

<h2>Literature</h2>
<p>Porreca, David. "Hermes Trismegistus in the Latin Middle Ages." PhD dissertation. University of Waterloo, 2001.</p>
<p>Lucentini, Paolo and Mark D. Delp. <i>Hermetis Trismegisti De sex rerum principiis</i>. Turnhout: Brepols, 2006.</p>
<p>Hanegraaff, Wouter J. (ed.). <i>Dictionary of Gnosis and Western Esotericism</i>. 2 vols. Leiden: Brill, 2006.</p>
<p>Burnett, Charles. "The Coherence of the Arabic-Latin Translation Program in Toledo in the Twelfth Century." <i>Science in Context</i> 14 (2001): 249–288.</p>
<p>Copenhaver, Brian P. <i>Hermetica: The Greek Corpus Hermeticum and the Latin Asclepius</i>. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1992.</p>
<p>Dronke, Peter (ed.). <i>A History of Twelfth-Century Western Philosophy</i>. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1988.</p>
<p>Baeumker, Clemens. <i>Das pseudo-hermetische Buch der vierundzwanzig Meister (Liber XXIV philosophorum)</i>. Münster: Aschendorff, 1913.</p>"""

